{"subject":"Pathology","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Pathology::14.Classification_and_Features_of_Neoplasia","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530106","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old female presents with a palpable lump in her left breast which is freely mobile and painless. A biopsy of the lump was performed, and a histopathological examination was done. Which feature from the biopsy report is most likely to differentiate between benign and malignant breast lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of well-defined glandular structures"},{"label":"B","text":"Evidence of invasion into surrounding tissues"},{"label":"C","text":"Low mitotic rate"},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of fibrous stroma"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Evidence of invasion into surrounding tissues","solutionHtml":"\"f8aaf091221692777191f73e810e94\"&amp;gt;malignant tumours based on whether the basement <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> is intact or broken:\n\t<ul>\n<li>In <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1bbae82f1f16927771824f797639fa\">benign</span> tumours: The basement <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> is completely intact and normal.</li>\n<li>In <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f8aaf091221692777191f73e810e94\">malignant</span> tumours: The basement <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> is broken, so the cancer cells can invade surrounding tissues.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n<p> </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:624px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Benign Lesions</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Malignant Lesions</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Growth</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p>Localised</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p>Invasive<strong> (Option B)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Microscopic Appearance</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p>Resemble normal tissue and maintain differentiated features. <strong>(Option A)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Cellular atypia</strong>, mitotic figures, loss of tissue organisation.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Macroscopic Appearance</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p>Well-defined borders</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p>Irregular borders, necrosis, hemorrhage.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Histological Features</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p>Cells are typically uniform in size and shape, with minimal nuclear <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"79886dc4391692777182791548ca21\">atypia</span> and low mitotic rate. <strong>(Option C)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p>Cells may display pleomorphism, increased nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, high mitotic rate, and evidence of invasion or metastasis.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotran&#39;s Pathologic basis of disease 10th edition, page 268</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530108","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":2,"stem":"Which of the following tumour is of mesenchymal origin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Papilloma"},{"label":"C","text":"Seminoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Leiomyoma"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Leiomyoma","solutionHtml":"span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1bbae82f1f16927771824f797639fa\"&amp;gt;benign tumour of mesenchymal origin, originating from the smooth muscles of the uterus.\n<p><strong>Adenoma (Option A): </strong>Arises from gland: <strong>Epithelial Tumour</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Papilloma (Option B): </strong>Usually arises from surfaces like skin: <strong>Epithelial Tumour</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Seminoma (Option C): </strong>Germ cell tumour in testes: <strong>Epithelial Tumour</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:624px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:bottom\"> </td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Mesenchymal Tumours</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-left:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-right:1px solid #0d0d0d; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Epithelial Tumours</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Origin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Connective tissues and derivatives</li>\n<li>Blood and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> vessels</li>\n<li>Hematopoietic stem cells and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"32599f889a169277719169e3483e62\">lymphoid</span> tissue</li>\n<li>Meninges</li>\n<li>Mesothelium</li>\n<li>Smooth muscles and Skeletal muscles</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #0d0d0d; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Stratified <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5be0ee4ea316927771997104326685\">squamous</span> epithelium</li>\n<li>Melanocytes and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"923af580cf16927771829606bce2a7\">basal</span> cells of the skin</li>\n<li>Glandular <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"53782f88d31692777187177cb73b24\">epithelium</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"53782f88d31692777187177cb73b24\">epithelium</span> of ducts</li>\n<li>Respiratory, Hepatic, Renal, Transitional, Testicular, Placental epithelium</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Examples</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Leiomyosarcoma</li>\n<li>Lipoma</li>\n<li>Osteosarcoma</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyosarcoma</li>\n<li>Fibrosarcoma</li>\n<li>Meningioma</li>\n<li>Synovial sarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Squamous cell carcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Adenoma</strong><strong> </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Papilloma</strong><strong> </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Seminoma</strong></li>\n<li>Hepatocellular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Transitional cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Markers</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Vimentin</li>\n<li>Desmin</li>\n<li>S100 Protein</li>\n<li>Fibronectin</li>\n<li>Myogenin</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cytokeratin</li>\n<li>Epithelial Membrane Antigen (EMA)</li>\n<li>E-Cadherin</li>\n<li>P63, P40</li>\n<li>Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease 10th edition, page 270</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530110","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":3,"stem":"What is the most likely diagnosis of a patient with a painless, slowly enlarging mass in the right parotid gland region that is well-defined and heterogeneous, with both epithelial and mesenchymal components present in the biopsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleomorphic adenoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Mucoepidermoid carcinoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Acinic cell carcinoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Warthin tumour"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Pleomorphic adenoma","solutionHtml":"lass=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c3c9726266169277718021f4f0fa16\"&amp;gt;adenoma is the most common <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1bbae82f1f16927771824f797639fa\">benign</span> tumour of the salivary glands, often found in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"42d0553c3916927771944110a6f15b\">parotid</span> gland.\n<li>Pleomorphic adenoma, also known as a mixed tumour, typically contains epithelial components (such as <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c2f9d01ba91692777188d264244b6a\">glandular</span> cells) and mesenchymal components (such as <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"58f239fc181692777193528b77cc8c\">myxoid</span> stroma, islands of cartilage, or bone).</li>\n<li>Pleomorphic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c3c9726266169277718021f4f0fa16\">adenoma</span> is more commonly found in females.</li>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Dr Saranya Singaravel\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://ilovepathology.com/pathology-of-pleomorphic-adenoma/\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"2112\" src=\"images/m51h54Oqv1O5Ym642Tit1736952266.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p><br/><img src=\"images/m51h54Oqv1O5Ym642Tit1736952266.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic Basis of Disease 10th edition, page 269</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530112","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":4,"stem":"A 30-year-old female presents with pelvic pain and irregular menstrual cycles. She undergoes surgical resection of the mass, and histopathological examination reveals a tumour composed of mature tissues derived from multiple germ layers, including hair, teeth, and sebaceous material. Based on the histopathological findings described, which of the following tumours is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teratoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Serous cystadenoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Mucinous cystadenoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Granulosa cell tumour"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Teratoma","solutionHtml":"from 2 or more <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b3623bc67216927771889eb144d9ed\">germ</span> layers\n<li>Teratomas are commonly seen in the ovaries, but can also be seen in other organs of the body.</li>\n<li>Teratomas are of 2 types:\n\t<ul>\n<li><strong>Mature teratoma:</strong> Benign and has all the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0472620c171692777192c655af9c57\">mature</span> tissues</li>\n<li><strong>Immature teratoma:</strong> Has <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7b4f6b179a16927771930ad6ac3581\">neuroepithelial</span> cells and has the potential to cause cancer.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n\n<p><img alt=\"Teratoma Gross \" data-author=\"Ed Uthman\" data-hash=\"5501\" data-license=\"CC BY 2.0\" data-source=\"https://eml.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mature_Cystic_Teratoma_of_the_Ovary_%285560431170%29.jpg\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"387\" src=\"images/6TuLLFl1c3zq9etQjN2s1743432373.png\"\" width=\"500\"/></p><br/><img src=\"images/6TuLLFl1c3zq9etQjN2s1743432373.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease, 10th edition, Page 269</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530114","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":5,"stem":"A 55-year-old man presents with chronic heartburn and difficulty swallowing. Upon examination, the physician suspects gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) and orders an upper GI endoscopic biopsy, which reveals the presence of columnar epithelium in the lower oesophagus, replacing the normal squamous epithelium. Which of the following terms describes the observed cellular change?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metaplasia"},{"label":"B","text":"Dysplasia"},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplasia"},{"label":"D","text":"Desmoplasia"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Metaplasia","solutionHtml":"defined as the replacement of one type of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"53782f88d31692777187177cb73b24\">epithelium</span> with another.\n<li>Metaplasia is 100% reversible.\n\t<ul>\n<li>Until the trigger (GERD in this case) is present, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cb4dfcbab11692777192d282b3be7a\">metaplasia</span> will stay, and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cb4dfcbab11692777192d282b3be7a\">metaplasia</span> will be reversed when the trigger is treated.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1c785706ff169277720041cddf2d01\">terminology</span> of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cb4dfcbab11692777192d282b3be7a\">metaplasia</span> is based on the end <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"53782f88d31692777187177cb73b24\">epithelium</span> it forms.</li>\n<li>Intestinal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8ff554096316927771851cc310435e\">columnar epithelium</span> has goblet cells. Acidic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7d30ef9aee16927771936bdac2c1ff\">mucin</span> is present inside goblet cells, which is Alcian blue positive.</li>\n<li>Barrett's <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6a729a105a169277718788389dbeab\">esophagus</span> is a precancerous condition, and if not treated, it may turn into Adenocarcinoma of esophagus.\n\t<ul>\n</ul>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Prasad G. Iyer,Vivek Kaul\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"© 2019 Mayo Foundation for Medical Education and Research\" data-source=\"https://www.mayoclinicproceedings.org/article/S0025-6196(19)30124-7/fulltext#fig2\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"3271\" src=\"images/wj0t8UYoO9CZnVsVi3yd1736952540.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n</li>\n<br/><img src=\"images/wj0t8UYoO9CZnVsVi3yd1736952540.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease, 10th edition, Pages 270-272</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530116","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":6,"stem":"Which of the following features is not a characteristic of anaplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleomorphism"},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of polarity"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio"},{"label":"D","text":"Intact basement membrane"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Intact basement membrane","solutionHtml":"based on the basement membrane.\n<ul>\n<li>If the basement <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> is intact: <strong>Precancer/Dysplasia</strong></li>\n<li>If the basement <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> is broken or breached: Cancer cells can invade into surrounding tissue and spread anywhere:  <strong>Cancer/Anaplaisa</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Ed Uthman\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY-SA 2.0 \" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripolar_Mitosis_-_breast_carcinoma.jpg\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"988\" src=\"images/SHHtD0lzN7fkUm8Eua7o1736952938.png\"\" width=\"1308\"/></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Image showing Mitosis: Mercedes Benz sign</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Taryn A. Donovan https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5740-9550 taryn.donovan@amcny.org, Frances M. Moore, Christof A. Bertram https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2402-9997, Richard Luong, Pompei Bolfa, Robert Klopfleisch https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6308-0568, Harold Tvedten, Elisa N. Salas, Derick B. Whitley, Marc Aubreville, and Donald J. Meuten\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0300985820980049\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"807\" src=\"images/r6qcQVWmaTwcNngUGtoJ1736953094.png\"\" width=\"804\"/></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Image showing Mitosis: Starburst mitosis</strong></p><br/><img src=\"images/SHHtD0lzN7fkUm8Eua7o1736952938.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/r6qcQVWmaTwcNngUGtoJ1736953094.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease 10th edition, Page 270-272</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530118","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":7,"stem":"Which of the following statements is/are True? The basement membrane remains intact in dysplasia. Dysplasia is always irreversible. Pleomorphism is observed in metaplasia. Myositis ossificans is an example of dysplasia.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 and 4"},{"label":"B","text":"1 only"},{"label":"C","text":"2 and 4"},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"1 only","solutionHtml":"0; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\"&amp;gt;\n\t\t\t<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Metaplasia </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Dysplasia</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Anaplasia </strong></p>\n</td>\n\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Definition</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Differentiated cell</p>\n<p>type (epithelial or mesenchymal) is replaced by another cell type</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Disordered growth and loss of architectural orientation of cells</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Lack of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"12a6f23332169277718402ca6703e2\">cellular</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"55a682d72e16927771865bb4855ba3\">differentiation</span> and organisation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Nature <strong>(Statement 2)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Adaptive response to stress or injury</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Preneoplastic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Associated with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f8aaf091221692777191f73e810e94\">malignant</span> transformation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Reversibility</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Reversible</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Reversible in early stages</p>\n<p>(irreversible if whole</p>\n<p>the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"53782f88d31692777187177cb73b24\">epithelium</span> is involved)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Irreversible</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Pleomorphism <strong>(Statement 3)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Absent</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Present in low-grade</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Present in high-grade</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Normal (1:4 to 1:6)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Increased (↑)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Increased (↑↑↑)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Mitotic figures</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Absent/minimal at normal places</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Typical <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e0da80f7b91692777192668c299922\">mitotic figures</span> present at <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"458e050fdc16927771809854c9fd53\">abnormal</span> places</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Atypical, bizarre mitotic figures</p>\n<p>present at <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"458e050fdc16927771809854c9fd53\">abnormal</span> places</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Basement <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> integrity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Intact</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Intact</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Breached</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Example<strong> (Statement 4)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Barret’s oesophagus,</p>\n<p>myositis ossificans</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Cervical <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"984733db481692777186757cce6e6b\">dysplasia</span> of</p>\n<p>squamous cells</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Carcinoma of the cervix, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"988c19e3131692777183b6744d1920\">carcinoma</span> oesophagus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease 10th edition, Page 270-272</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530120","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":8,"stem":"Which of the following characteristics distinguishes hemangiomas from other benign tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid growth rate"},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of extracellular matrix (ECM) deposited by stromal cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Easily resected by excision"},{"label":"D","text":"Unencapsulated"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Unencapsulated","solutionHtml":"stomMeta\" data-dictid=\"02b3e74f2c1692777188c83f24c1ad\"&amp;gt;hemangioma is a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1bbae82f1f16927771824f797639fa\">benign</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"526ede3fee169277720184378acc5e\">vascular</span> tumour caused by an <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"458e050fdc16927771809854c9fd53\">abnormal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2840cd55771692777194448139206d\">overgrowth</span> of blood vessels that is distinguished from other <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1bbae82f1f16927771824f797639fa\">benign</span> tumours by:\n<ul>\n<li>Lack of well-defined <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"54635b1bf01692777183247ecff5e0\">capsule</span> (Hemangiomas are uncapsulated).</li>\n<li>Tendency to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7fefe2d95c16927771956007239d62\">permeate</span> the site of origin making it difficult for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"81d12f9cb01692777199e44ff3f3c1\">surgical</span> excision.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Rapid growth rate (Option A):</strong></p>\n<p>The rapid growth rate is a feature of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f8aaf091221692777191f73e810e94\">malignant</span> tumors, not <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1bbae82f1f16927771824f797639fa\">benign</span> tumors which grow slowly and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8fb8df140b1692777191702bbc9358\">localized</span> to the site of origin.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Presence of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ba366417791692777187229d34e597\">extracellular</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"14958c535b1692777192abd2a85abf\">matrix</span> (ECM) deposited by stromal cells (Option B):</strong></p>\n<p>Benign tumours usually has a capsule, which consists of an <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ba366417791692777187229d34e597\">extracellular</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"14958c535b1692777192abd2a85abf\">matrix</span> (ECM) deposited by stromal cells such as fibroblasts.</p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"02b3e74f2c1692777188c83f24c1ad\">hemangioma</span> is unencapsulated, it is not associated with the deposition of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ba366417791692777187229d34e597\">extracellular</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"14958c535b1692777192abd2a85abf\">matrix</span> by stromal cells.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Easily resected by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"20b3ce06c5169277718756ee95c1e9\">excision</span> (Option C):</strong></p>\n<p>Encapsulation makes <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1bbae82f1f16927771824f797639fa\">benign</span> tumours easily <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e93e54afd9169277719731c7b12d94\">resectable</span> but since hemangiomas are unencapsulated ,they <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7fefe2d95c16927771956007239d62\">permeate</span> site of origin making it difficult to resect.</p>\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease 10th edition, page 273</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530122","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":9,"stem":"Which of the following types of cancer is least likely to exhibit distant metastasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal cell carcinoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Melanoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Basal cell carcinoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Prostatic carcinoma"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Basal cell carcinoma","solutionHtml":"<strong>Explanation: </strong>\n<p>Metastasis means the distant spread of cancer.</p>\n<p>All cancers are capable of showing <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d3251bfb0b1692777192b069d7ba4a\">metastasis</span> except 2 cancers, which show local invasion:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Basal cell cancer/Rodent's ulcer, which occurs on face.</li>\n<li>Gliomas: Brain tumour.</li>\n</ol><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease 10th edition, page 273, 274</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530124","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":10,"stem":"Which of the following tumours do not typically exhibit direct seeding of body cavities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudomyxoma peritonei"},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric carcinoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Medulloblastoma"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","solutionHtml":"s=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"988c19e3131692777183b6744d1920\"&amp;gt;carcinoma typically exhibits <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d3251bfb0b1692777192b069d7ba4a\">metastasis</span> by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"33bb6837aa169277718854a46f5cbd\">hematogenous</span> spread rather than direct seeding of body cavities.\n\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Direct Seeding of Body Cavities or Surfaces:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pseudomyxoma peritonei <strong>(Option A)</strong> is characterized by the presence of a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"be6fe9c6c316927771889dbaaf2b89\">gelatinous</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c82360aadd16927771936d67fa30a4\">neoplastic</span> mass <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"52edf0af4d1692777202dd1a490884\">filling</span> the <strong>peritoneal cavity</strong> arising from the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e73553a3a5169277719787fd5f4680\">rupture</span> of mucin-secreting <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"362992dbb11692777180f9017fc04d\">adenocarcinoma</span> of the appendix (most common cause) or mucin-secreting <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"362992dbb11692777180f9017fc04d\">adenocarcinoma</span> of ovarian.</li>\n<li>Gastric <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"988c19e3131692777183b6744d1920\">carcinoma</span> <strong>(Option B) </strong>can spread to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5ff25303a01692777186731f6a4f5c\">distal</span> end of the peritoneal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ddba4206eb169277718335596e86da\">cavity</span> (Pouch of Douglas) and is known as the ‘Blummer shelf.’</li>\n<li>Medulloblastoma <strong>(Option D)</strong> from the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a9c41a58d816927771847775d63d96\">cerebellum</span> spreads to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d816ba952f1692777199fc69bd7c56\">spinal</span> cord by ‘Drop metastasis.’</li>\n</ol><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease, 10th edition, Pages 273, 274</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530126","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":11,"stem":"Which of the following tumours does not follow the lymphatic metastatic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhabdomyosarcoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Krukenberg tumor"},{"label":"C","text":"Follicular thyroid carcinoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Synovial sarcoma"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Follicular thyroid carcinoma","solutionHtml":"le=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\"&amp;gt;\n\t\t\t<p><strong>Hematogenous spread</strong></p>\n\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Lymphatic spread</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Transcoelomic spread</strong></p>\n</td>\n\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Veins are more <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"607476ab371692777196d0cb2f13bc\">prone</span> to invasion than arteries due to their thinner walls.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Tumours use existing <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4fb6bd90f016927771918d4a2993ed\">lymphatic</span> vessels at their margins. Follows natural routes of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4fb6bd90f016927771918d4a2993ed\">lymphatic</span> drainage.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Invades the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5ce9b6c57f1692777198cdc854b76a\">serosal</span> wall of the coelomic cavity, releasing tumour fragments into coelomic fluid, which is implanted elsewhere in the body cavity.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #6aa84f; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Seen in most of the sarcomas.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #93c47d; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Seen in most carcinomas.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #93c47d; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Krukenberg tumour is a metastatic tumour to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7b1a7ff355169277719401475de6fb\">ovary</span> that can show <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4fb6bd90f016927771918d4a2993ed\">lymphatic</span> or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b87d9415f61692777200f0a960d9fa\">transcoelomic</span> spread. <strong>(Option B)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Exceptions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Synovial <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"82068425041692777198fe3064c370\">sarcoma</span> <strong>(Option D)</strong></li>\n<li>Rhabdomyosarcoma <strong>(Option A)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/>\n\t\t\tThese tumours are spread by the <strong>lymphatic </strong>route.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Exceptions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>HCC</li>\n<li>RCC</li>\n<li>Follicular <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"988c19e3131692777183b6744d1920\">carcinoma</span> of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"799202e6fe16927772009a1501b0a8\">thyroid</span> <strong>(Option C)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/>\n\t\t\tThese tumours are spread by a <strong>hematogenous </strong>route.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\"> </td>\n</tr>\n\n\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease 10th edition, page 274, 275</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436530128","topic":"PrepLadder · 14.Classification and Features of Neoplasia","number":12,"stem":"Which of the following is true regarding the Sentinel lymph node?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The first lymph node to receive metastasis from a secondary tumour."},{"label":"B","text":"Also helpful in identifying metastasis in Melanoma."},{"label":"C","text":"Commonly involved in skip metastasis"},{"label":"D","text":"The presence of cancer cells indicates a good prognosis"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Also helpful in identifying metastasis in Melanoma.","solutionHtml":"2fd1af767\"&amp;gt;lymph <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> (SLN): The first <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> to receive <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"dd824a31e71692777186c7f8168998\">drainage</span> from a primary tumor site.\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Example: In breast cancer, usually axillary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> is the first <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> where the lymphatics are drained. Hence, it is the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f08132bdfb16927771986a66c5d7b6\">sentinel</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> node.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Sometimes, cancer may <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4fc6b3ee501692777183061d4f55ff\">bypass</span> the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f08132bdfb16927771986a66c5d7b6\">sentinel</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> and spread directly to other <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> nodes. This can occur in patients who have received radiotherapy, as radiation can cause <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"21e2d5a70616927772021fcdec0ca9\">fibrosis</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"43c30dc8c8169277718317ffb7dea0\">blockage</span> of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4fb6bd90f016927771918d4a2993ed\">lymphatic</span> channels. If the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3aa5abc2fa169277719488e088076d\">pathway</span> to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f08132bdfb16927771986a66c5d7b6\">sentinel</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> is obstructed, tumour cells are diverted to alternative routes, resulting in what is known as <strong>skip metastasis</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Tumors where SLN <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"754ab07a2e1692777183ed178c883d\">biopsy</span> is validated are Breast cancer, Melanoma, Vulvar cancer, and Colon cancer.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The first <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> to receive <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d3251bfb0b1692777192b069d7ba4a\">metastasis</span> from a secondary tumour (Option A): </strong>Sentinal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> is the first <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> to receive <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"dd824a31e71692777186c7f8168998\">drainage</span> from a primary tumor site.</p>\n<p><strong>Commonly involved in skip <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d3251bfb0b1692777192b069d7ba4a\">metastasis</span> (Option C):</strong> Skip <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d3251bfb0b1692777192b069d7ba4a\">metastasis</span> is not a very common phenomenon; it's rather rare.</p>\n<p><strong>The presence of cancer cells indicates a good <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e580b4033e169277719609af594c16\">prognosis</span> (Option D):</strong> Cancer <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ad56eb6e551692777199ae4ce59798\">spreading</span> to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f08132bdfb16927771986a66c5d7b6\">sentinel</span> or any other <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4df97a6cbf169277719172fd1af767\">lymph</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b10ce4acbf169277719323ffd12306\">node</span> is always a bad prognosis.</p>\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Robbins and Cotrans Pathologic basis of disease 10th edition, page 275</div>","tags":""}]}